make re
UsageThe program requires exactly 8 mandatory arguments passed via the command line:Bash./codexion <number_of_coders> <time_to_burnout> <time_to_compile> <time_to_debug> <time_to_refactor> <number_of_compiles_required> <dongle_cooldown> <scheduler>
ArgumentsArgumentDescriptionnumber_of_codersTotal number of coders at the table (and the number of available dongles).time_to_burnoutMaximum time (ms) a coder can go without starting a compilation before burning out.time_to_compileTime duration (ms) required to perform a quantum compilation.time_to_debugTime duration (ms) spent in the debugging phase.time_to_refactorTime duration (ms) spent in the refactoring phase.number_of_compiles_requiredNumber of successful compiles each individual coder must complete for a clean exit.dongle_cooldownMandatory cooling period (ms) a dongle must undergo after release before being claimed again.schedulerThe arbitration scheduling algorithm used to handle simultaneous requests (fifo or edf).Execution ExampleBash./codexion 4 410 200 200 200 5 10 edf
Expected standard output layout:Plaintext0 1 has taken a dongle
0 1 has taken a dongle
0 1 is compiling
200 1 is debugging
400 1 is refactoring
401 2 has taken a dongle
402 2 has taken a dongle
402 2 is compiling
1204 3 burned out
Scheduler PoliciesDongle allocation under high resource contention is managed using a priority queue built as a binary Min-Heap (t_queue and t_node). Sorting keys inside the heap adapt depending on the specified policy:FIFO (First In, First Out)Logic: Dongles are granted in the strict chronological order in which coders submit their requests.Priority: The priority_value field inside the node represents the timestamp of the resource request.Characteristics: Simple, predictable, and fair ordering based entirely on arrival times.EDF (Earliest Deadline First)Logic: Dongles are prioritized to the coder whose starvation threshold is closest to expiring.Deadline Formula: $\text{deadline} = \text{last\_meal} + \text{time\_to\_burnout}$Characteristics: Heavily reduces burnout rates under load by pulling the most critical thread straight to the root of the heap.Blocking Cases HandledDeadlock PreventionTo completely eliminate circular wait scenarios (reminiscent of the Dining Philosophers problem where every thread picks up one resource and hangs indefinitely), take_dongles in routine.c enforces an asymmetric acquisition strategy based on ID parity (coder->id):Even-indexed coders lock their right dongle first, and then their left dongle.Odd-indexed coders lock their left dongle first, and then their right dongle.Starvation PreventionThe structural fairness of the FIFO queue and the urgency-driven design of the EDF scheduler guarantee that no thread is pushed aside indefinitely. Under heavy contention, EDF explicitly rescues threads on the verge of collapsing.Dongle Cooldown EnforcementWhen a dongle is dropped, its release timestamp is stored in last_released within t_dongle. Incoming resource requests cross-examine this timestamp against the current time. If the target hardware hasn't cleared its cooldown period yet, the requesting thread is safely put to sleep.Accurate Burnout DetectionTime monitoring is decoupled from the coder loop. An independent monitor thread continually runs monitor_routine, measuring the real-time gap since each coder's last productive cycle (last_meal). If a coder expires, the monitor safely triggers the stop_sim flag under stop_mutex protection, bringing all loops to an immediate halt.Serialized LoggingTo avoid mixed, fragmented, or garbled terminal messages resulting from concurrent standard output writes, all log emissions route through print_status, which is fully synchronized by a centralized log_mutex.Thread Synchronization MechanismsStrict state safety and the prevention of data races are achieved using the following pthread primitives:pthread_mutex_t:Dongle Mutexes: Each t_dongle structure holds its own exclusive lock to safeguard its status, assignment flags, and min-heap arrays.log_mutex: Guarantees atomicity when printing logs to stdout.stop_mutex: Protects reading and writing operations for the global simulation state tracker (stop_sim).pthread_cond_t: Tied to individual dongles to block waiting threads efficiently instead of wasting CPU cycles (busy-waiting). Threads sleep passively until woken up via controlled broadcasting (pthread_cond_broadcast / signal) triggered by hardware release or cooldown completions.Timing & Memory ManagementPrecision Timing: The program uses gettimeofday() to capture time metrics with millisecond-level fidelity. To bypass the scheduling inaccuracies inherent to standard usleep(), a custom smart_sleep() utility is implemented. It slices delays into tiny microsecond chunks while continuously polling the global simulation state.Memory Management: The application strictly follows 42's zero-leak requirements. The cleanup_all function inside init.c cleanly handles the destruction of all active variables of condition, destroys every initialized mutex, and frees the dynamic arrays allocated on the heap (nodes, dongles, coders), ensuring zero memory leaks upon termination.Allowed FunctionsThis project is built strictly using the functions explicitly permitted by the project specification:Plaintextpthread_create          pthread_cond_destroy      free
pthread_join            gettimeofday              printf
pthread_mutex_init      usleep                    fprintf
pthread_mutex_lock      write                     strcmp
pthread_mutex_unlock    malloc                    strlen
pthread_mutex_destroy                             atoi
pthread_cond_init                                 memset
pthread_cond_wait
pthread_cond_timedwait
pthread_cond_broadcast
AI Usage DisclosureIn compliance with the academic integrity guidelines set forth in Chapter II of the project specification, AI tools were utilized during development strictly as productivity and scaffolding aids to eliminate mechanical tasks.Specifically, AI was used to automate the layout structure of this documentation file (README.md), draft initial unit testing cases, and generate repetitive boilerplate structures for struct initializations. The core architecture design, the binary min-heap pointer logic, the asymmetric deadlock-breaking locking sequences, and the thread-safe timing boundaries were wholly conceived, manually written, and peer-reviewed within the 42 community, with full technical ownership and complete understanding of the delivered source code.Learning OutcomesAdvanced understanding of multithreading in UNIX systems using the pthread library.Hands-on implementation and algorithmic evaluation of real-time task schedulers (FIFO and EDF).Concrete experience preventing classical concurrency hazards: Deadlocks (Coffman conditions), Data Races, and Starvation.Design and engineering of custom dynamic priority queues matching strict C89 restrictions.Precise clock synchronization and asynchronous state handling under tight timing constraints.LicenseThis project is part of the 42 curriculum and is designed strictly for educational and academic purposes.